    {
      "lens": "owner",
      "summary": "I walked through the site as Bill would: on a 390px iPhone frame (via _check.html at 2x) and at 1440px. I covered the hub, the AI agents page, the $1M page, the business case, and prototypes 01, 03 and 05 in both customer and ?view=team views, plus first screens of 02, 04, 06 and 07. I read the rendered text from headless-Chrome DOM dumps. No files were edited.\n\nThe prototypes look strong and feel like J&W: real products and prices, the J&W logo and navy palette, and convincing team consoles. What lets Bill down is the wrapper around them:\n- **The first thing he reads is broken on a phone.** The H1 shows \"butcherinto\".\n- **The first screen has no clear starting step.**\n- **There is no way to actually pick prototypes.** The \"Email us your shortlist\" link opens an empty email with only a subject line.\n- **The pages disagree on what \"the plan\" is.** The hub says the plan starts with three plays but never names them. The business case assumes five, and the $1M page's path is three different ones. The $1M page itself gives $1.2M, $1.3M and $1.5M for the same path.\n- **The $1M page grades the plays he is meant to pick with school letters.** The start-this-month Gift Studio gets a C−, and two prototypes get a D.\n\nOn a phone, the AI agents page runs about 35 screens and is full of technical terms (FTE, SKUs, tokens, model IDs, Shopify Admin API), and its 10x table hides the \"Multiple\" column off-screen. The \"Why this makes money\" drawer opens on a dense block of formulas in a code font. Inside the prototypes, the sticky bars take about a fifth of the screen.\n\nTwo tone risks: his wife and son, named from Yelp and Chamber reviews, are given approval jobs throughout. The published audit also calls the family a \"key-person risk\" and names his son as the \"next-gen face\".\n\nSmaller problems: double brackets on all 7 \"Modelled on\" lines, a large empty area next to the \"Your turn\" card on desktop, a cut-off strip at the bottom of the flagship screenshot, and a few figures and labels that don't match between pages.\n\nRanked findings below.",
      "findings": [
        {
          "page": "index.html (hub)",
          "where": "Hero H1, first thing on screen; iPhone 390px and desktop 1440px",
          "issue": "On iPhone the headline reads \"Seven ways to turn Scarborough's 4.8-star butcherinto the GTA's protein brand\": the words run together. On desktop the forced line break leaves \"star butcher\" alone on line 2 (\"4.8-\" / \"star butcher\" / \"into the GTA's protein brand\"). The title was made longer but the hard break was kept.",
          "evidence": "index.html:182 runs esc(H.title).replace(/\\|/g, '<br class=\"hidden md:block\">'). The <br> is display:none below md and has no space next to it. site-data.js:29 title = \"...4.8-star butcher|into the GTA's protein brand\". Seen in the 390px screenshot and the 1440px screenshot.",
          "fix": "Remove the forced break and let the browser balance the lines. In index.html:182 change it to: document.getElementById('hero-h').textContent = H.title.replace(/\\|/g, ' '); and add style=\"text-wrap:balance\" to the h1#hero-h. If the break must stay, at least use .replace(/\\|/g, ' <br class=\"hidden lg:block\">'), with a space before the <br>.",
          "severity": "high"
        },
        {
          "page": "index.html (hub)",
